    {
      "id": "recap",
      "seconds": 10,
      "heading": "Three kinds of loop",
      "narration": "In the last episode, we compared a loop with a whole array operation. This time, we turn common loops into array operations. Three kinds come up again and again. Map, filter, and reduce.",
      "lines": [],
      "slide": {
        "type": "points",
        "steps": [
          0,
          0,
          0,
          3
        ],
        "items": [
          {
            "text": "Map",
            "sub": "every element, transformed"
          },
          {
            "text": "Filter",
            "sub": "some elements, kept"
          },
          {
            "text": "Reduce",
            "sub": "many elements, combined into one"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "loop",
      "seconds": 15,
      "heading": "One loop, three jobs",
      "narration": "Here is a small Rust loop. It takes the values two, four, six, eight. It triples each value. It keeps the ones greater than five. And it adds up what it keeps. Three jobs are tangled together in one loop body.",
      "lines": [],
      "panel": {
        "type": "code",
        "lines": [
          {
            "code": "let values = [2, 4, 6, 8];"
          },
          {
            "code": "let mut sum = 0;"
          },
          {
            "code": "for x in values {"
          },
          {
            "code": "    let y = x * 3;"
          },
          {
            "code": "    if y > 5 { sum += y; }"
          },
          {
            "code": "}"
          }
        ],
        "focus": [
          [],
